          "id": "thm-first-possible-complex-k-ahss-differential-is-integral-sq-three",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "proof_plan": "The displayed bidegree sends an even coefficient row under d₂ to an odd coefficient group, which is zero, so d₂=0 and E₃=E₂. First handle p=0, where the positive-degree Postnikov argument does not apply. A finite CW complex has finitely many path components. On each component X_a, every x∈H^0(X_a;Z) is the pullback of an integer along X_a→*. AHSS naturality gives d₃(x) as the pullback of d₃ on the point, whose H^3 target is zero; restriction to all components therefore gives d₃(x)=0. Instability also gives Sq²ρ₂(x)=0, so β_ZSq²ρ₂(x)=0. For p≥1, Bott-translate the coefficient row to q≤0. The preceding representability/comparison lemma identifies the local K-AHSS d₃ with the first representing-space ku Postnikov obstruction, including the p=1 suspension reduction and the finite-pair case. The connective-k-invariant lemma identifies that obstruction with β_ZSq²ρ₂. Transport back through the fixed natural Bott isomorphisms; naturality for theory morphisms makes these isomorphisms maps of skeletal exact couples. A possible global sign from the long-exact-sequence convention is immaterial because the operation has order two.",
          "provenance": {
            "proof": "ai-altered",
            "statement": "literature-derived"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "locator": "Proposition 16.6 and its proof, printed pp. 391–393",
                "title": "J. F. Adams, Stable Homotopy and Generalised Homology",
                "url": "https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/otherpapers/Adams-SHGH-latex2.pdf"
