                "url": "https://www.math.columbia.edu/~calebji/atiyah-hirzebruch-final.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume AC. In the complex K-AHSS, d_2=0 and d_3=Sq_Z^3=βSq²ρ_2, with Bott-periodic translates on all even coefficient rows.",
          "title": "The first possible complex K-theory AHSS differential is integral Sq-three"
        },
        {
          "deps": [
            "def-bockstein-connecting-operation",
            "prop-first-steenrod-square-is-the-mod-two-bockstein"
          ],
          "id": "lem-reduction-of-the-integral-bockstein-is-the-first-steenrod-square",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "proof_plan": "Represent x by a mod-two cocycle and choose an integral lift c. Write δc=2b, so β_Z(x) is represented by b and its reduction by b mod 2. Reducing c mod 4 gives the same quotient δc/2 mod 2 that defines the Bockstein for 0→Z/2→Z/4→Z/2→0. The published identification of that Bockstein with Sq¹ yields the formula; changing the lift changes b mod 2 by a coboundary.",
          "provenance": {
            "proof": "ai-altered",
            "statement": "literature-derived"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "locator": "§3.E, Bockstein homomorphisms, printed pp.303–305",
                "title": "Hatcher, Algebraic Topology",
                "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "For the integral Bockstein β_Z:H^n(X;F_2)→H^{n+1}(X;Z), reduction modulo two satisfies ρ_2β_Z(x)=Sq¹(x) for every space X and every n≥0. No choice principle is needed.",
          "title": "Reduction of the integral Bockstein is the first Steenrod square"
        }
      ]
    }

