          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Measure-theoretic mutual information",
          "statement": "Let $(E,\\mathcal E)$ and $(F,\\mathcal F)$ be measurable spaces and let $P$ be a probability measure on $(E\\times F,\\mathcal E\\otimes\\mathcal F)$. Its marginals are $P_E(A)=P(A\\times F)$ and $P_F(B)=P(E\\times B)$. The product probability $P_E\\otimes P_F$ exists by the finite product-measure theorem. For the absolutely continuous branch $P\\ll P_E\\otimes P_F$, assume AC as required by [[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]; the non-absolutely-continuous branch below remains $+\\infty$. Define the **measure-theoretic mutual information** of the joint law $P$ by\n$$I(P):=D(P\\|P_E\\otimes P_F)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor random elements $X,Y$ with joint law $P_{XY}$, write $I(X;Y):=I(P_{XY})$. The logarithm convention is the natural logarithm, so finite values are in nats as fixed by [[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]]. The divergence is directional in its two law arguments and is $+\\infty$ when $P_{XY}\\not\\ll P_X\\otimes P_Y$.\n\nThis definition uses only the joint law, not a choice of density or a difference of entropy quantities.",
