        {
          "id": "def-finite-f-divergence",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite f-divergence with support conventions",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-probability-vector",
            "def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets",
            "lem-convex-generator-boundary-limits-exist"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_obligations": [
            "State the convex generator class and both boundary limits.",
            "Define zero and singular-support contributions without an ambiguous 0 times infinity."
          ],
          "statement": "Let $P=(p_x)$ and $Q=(q_x)$ be probability vectors on a finite nonempty\nalphabet $\\mathcal X$ ([[def-finite-probability-vector]]). A **convex\ngenerator** is a finite convex function $f:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ with\n$f(1)=0$; convexity uses the two-point inequality of\n[[def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets]]. Use its\nextended boundary values\n$$f(0):=\\lim_{t\\downarrow0}f(t),\\qquad f_\\infty:=\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\frac{f(t)}t.$$\nThese limits exist in $\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ by\n[[lem-convex-generator-boundary-limits-exist]]. Define the **finite\nf-divergence from $P$ to $Q$**\nby\n$$D_f(P\\|Q):=\\sum_{x:q_x>0}q_x f(p_x/q_x)+\nf_\\infty\\sum_{x:q_x=0}p_x.$$\nIn the first sum, if $p_x=0$, the term means $q_x f(0)$. A coordinate with\n$p_x=q_x=0$ contributes zero and is omitted. The second term is defined as\nzero when its coefficient $\\sum_{q_x=0}p_x$ is zero, even if\n$f_\\infty=+\\infty$; if that coefficient is positive and $f_\\infty=+\\infty$,\nthe divergence is $+\\infty$.\n\nThe boundary term is essential when $P\\not\\ll Q$. It is the recession\ncontribution of the generator and is not the value $f(0)$.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 7, §7.1, Definition 7.1",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Igal Sason and Sergio Verdú, f-Divergence Inequalities, §II-B, Definition 3 and equations (35)–(39)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1508.00335"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-finite-hypothesis-testing-beta",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite type-II testing function with explicit orientation",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-probability-vector",
            "def-finite-probability-space-and-event",
            "def-randomized-test",
            "def-test-size-and-power",
            "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
            "def-complete-ordered-field",
            "def-real-numbers",
            "thm-reals-ordered-field",
            "cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_obligations": [
            "Fix P as the null and Q as the alternative.",
            "Define the rejection function, type-I constraint, and type-II objective; show the infimum is a real number."
          ],
          "statement": "Let $P,Q$ be probability vectors on a finite nonempty alphabet $\\mathcal X$\n([[def-finite-probability-vector]]). They define finite probability spaces\nwith outcome weights $p_x$ and $q_x$, respectively\n([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]). For $h:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R$, write\n$E_Ph:=\\sum_xp_xh(x)$ and $E_Qh:=\\sum_xq_xh(x)$; these are the finite\nexpectations ([[def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space]]). Test the\nsimple null $H_0:P$ against the simple alternative $H_1:Q$. A randomized test is a function\n$\\phi:\\mathcal X\\to[0,1]$, where $\\phi(x)$ is the probability of rejecting\n$H_0$ after observing $x$; thus $1-\\phi(x)$ is the probability of accepting\n$H_0$ ([[def-randomized-test]]). Its type-I error is\n$\\sum_xp_x\\phi(x)$, and its type-II error is\n$\\sum_xq_x(1-\\phi(x))$ ([[def-test-size-and-power]]).\n\nFor $\\epsilon\\in[0,1]$, define the oriented type-II testing function\n$$\\beta_\\epsilon(P,Q):=\\inf_{\\substack{\\phi:\\mathcal X\\to[0,1]\\\\\\sum_xp_x\\phi(x)\\le\\epsilon}}\\sum_xq_x(1-\\phi(x)).$$\nHere the first argument is the null law and the second is the alternative\nlaw; $\\epsilon$ bounds rejection under the first law, while the objective is\nacceptance of the null under the second. The feasible set is nonempty\nbecause $\\phi=0$ is allowed, and every objective value lies in $[0,1]$, so\nthe infimum is a real number in that interval. In the\nNeyman–Pearson-region convention that constrains null acceptance below by\n$\\gamma$, this is the lower boundary value at $\\gamma=1-\\epsilon$.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 14, §14.1, Definition 14.1",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "George Deligiannidis, Foundations of Statistical Inference, §13.1.1, Definition 13.1",
                "url": "https://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/~deligian/pdf/sb21/sb21_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-finite-stochastic-kernel",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite stochastic kernels",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-probability-vector"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_obligations": [
            "Fix the input/output alphabets and the row-stochastic convention.",
            "Define the induced output law with the input-first kernel convention."
          ],
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal X,\\mathcal Y$ be finite nonempty sets. A **stochastic kernel**\nfrom $\\mathcal X$ to $\\mathcal Y$ is a matrix\n$K=(K(y\\mid x))_{x\\in\\mathcal X,y\\in\\mathcal Y}$ such that\n$K(y\\mid x)\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{y\\in\\mathcal Y}K(y\\mid x)=1$ for every\n$x\\in\\mathcal X$. The convention is input first in the condition and output\nfirst in the value $K(y\\mid x)$.\n\nFor a probability vector $P=(p_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$, the output vector\n$PK$ on $\\mathcal Y$ is\n$$(PK)_y:=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}p_xK(y\\mid x).$$\nIt is a probability vector because its coordinates are nonnegative and\n$\\sum_y(PK)_y=\\sum_xp_x\\sum_yK(y\\mid x)=\\sum_xp_x=1$.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 7, §§7.1–7.2",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tim van Erven and Peter Harremoës, Rényi Divergence and Kullback-Leibler Divergence, §II-B",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1206.2459"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-finite-kullback-leibler-divergence",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite Kullback–Leibler divergence",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-probability-vector",
            "def-finite-f-divergence",
            "def-natural-logarithm",
            "lem-finite-kl-generator-is-admissible"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "proof_obligations": [
            "State the natural-log convention and the p-positive/q-zero infinity branch.",
            "Identify KL with the f-divergence generated by t log t."
          ],
          "statement": "For probability vectors $P=(p_x)$ and $Q=(q_x)$ on the same finite\nnonempty alphabet, their **Kullback–Leibler divergence** (or **relative\nentropy**) in the stated direction from $P$ to $Q$ is\n$$D(P\\|Q):=\\sum_{x:p_x>0}p_x\\log\\frac{p_x}{q_x}.$$\nThe logarithm is natural. If $p_x>0$ and $q_x=0$ for at least one $x$, set\n$D(P\\|Q)=+\\infty$. If $p_x=0$, its contribution is zero, including when\n$q_x=0$; equivalently, $0\\log(0/q_x)=0$. When $P\\ll Q$, all ratios in the\nsum are positive and finite.\n\nThe generator $f(t)=t\\log t$ is admissible for\n[[def-finite-f-divergence]] by\n[[lem-finite-kl-generator-is-admissible]]. Its boundary values\n$f(0)=0$ and $f_\\infty=+\\infty$ make that f-divergence exactly the displayed\nKL formula, including the singular-support case.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 7, §7.1",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tim van Erven and Peter Harremoës, Rényi Divergence and Kullback-Leibler Divergence, §I-A and Theorem 5",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1206.2459"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-convex-perspective-is-subadditive",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The closed convex perspective is subadditive",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-f-divergence",
            "thm-finite-jensen-inequality-in-euclidean-space",
            "lem-convex-generator-boundary-limits-exist"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "proof_obligations": [
            "Define the perspective at (0,0), on the positive b-axis, and on the singular boundary b=0.",
            "Prove the finite-sum inequality for both positive total second coordinate and total second coordinate zero."
          ],
          "statement": "Let $f$ be a convex generator from [[def-finite-f-divergence]]. Define its\nclosed perspective on $a,b\\ge0$ by\n$$\n\\Pi_f(a,b):=\\begin{cases}\nb f(a/b),&b>0,\\ a>0,\\\\\nb f(0),&b>0,\\ a=0,\\\\\na f_\\infty,&b=0,\\ a>0,\\\\\n0,&a=b=0.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nFor every finite family $(a_i,b_i)\\in[0,\\infty)^2$,\n$$\\Pi_f\\!\\left(\\sum_i a_i,\\sum_i b_i\\right)\\le\\sum_i\\Pi_f(a_i,b_i).$$",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 7, §7.1",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Igal Sason and Sergio Verdú, f-Divergence Inequalities, §II-B, equations (35)–(39)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1508.00335"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-finite-renyi-divergence",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite Rényi divergence",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-probability-vector",
            "def-finite-kullback-leibler-divergence",
            "def-natural-logarithm",
            "def-real-power"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_obligations": [
            "State the order range, the order-one value, and support/zero conventions for orders below and above one.",
            "Define the zero-affinity case as extended value +infinity."
          ],
          "statement": "Let $P=(p_x)$ and $Q=(q_x)$ be probability vectors on the same finite\nnonempty alphabet. For order $\\alpha\\in(0,\\infty)$ with $\\alpha\\ne1$, put\n$$S_\\alpha(P,Q):=\\sum_{x:q_x>0}p_x^\\alpha q_x^{1-\\alpha}.$$\nIf $\\alpha>1$ and $p_x>0=q_x$ for some $x$, define\n$S_\\alpha(P,Q)=+\\infty$; if $0<\\alpha<1$, such coordinates contribute\nzero. In the displayed sum, $p_x=0<q_x$ contributes zero. If the sum is\nzero, then necessarily $\\alpha<1$ and the logarithmic expression below is\ndefined as $+\\infty$. The **Rényi divergence of order $\\alpha$ from $P$ to\n$Q$** is\n$$D_\\alpha(P\\|Q):=\\frac{\\log S_\\alpha(P,Q)}{\\alpha-1}.$$\nAt order one define\n$$D_1(P\\|Q):=D(P\\|Q),$$\nusing [[def-finite-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. All divergences are\nextended-real-valued; the direction is always first argument to second.\n\nFor $0<\\alpha<1$, a point with $p_x>0=q_x$ contributes zero to the affinity\n$S_\\alpha$ rather than infinity. For $\\alpha>1$, the same support mismatch\nmakes $D_\\alpha(P\\|Q)=+\\infty$. The sign of $\\alpha-1$ is part of the\ndefinition and reverses inequalities when $0<\\alpha<1$.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 7, §7.12, Definition 7.22",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tim van Erven and Peter Harremoës, Rényi Divergence and Kullback-Leibler Divergence, §II-A, Definition 2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1206.2459"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-finite-iid-log-likelihood-concentrates",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Finite iid log-likelihood averages concentrate",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-probability-vector",
            "def-finite-kullback-leibler-divergence",
            "def-natural-logarithm",
            "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
            "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "proof_obligations": [
            "Assume P is absolutely continuous with respect to Q on the finite alphabet.",
            "Use boundedness on supp(P), independence, variance additivity, and Chebyshev's inequality."
          ],
          "statement": "Let $P=(p_x)$ and $Q=(q_x)$ be probability vectors on a finite nonempty\nalphabet with $P\\ll Q$. For $n\\ge1$, write\n$P^n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\prod_i p_{x_i}$ and\n$Q^n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\prod_i q_{x_i}$. Let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be independent\nwith common law $P$, define $\\ell(x)=\\log(p_x/q_x)$ when $p_x>0$ and\n$\\ell(x)=0$ when $p_x=0$, and put $L_n=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell(X_i)$. Then\n$L_n/n\\to D(P\\|Q)$ in probability as $n\\to\\infty$. For every\n$x^n\\in\\operatorname{supp}(P)^n$, one also has\n$Q^n(x^n)=P^n(x^n)\\exp(-L_n(x^n))$.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 14, §14.5, proof of Theorem 14.13",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-finite-f-divergence-data-processing",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Finite data processing for f-divergences",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-probability-vector",
            "def-finite-stochastic-kernel",
            "def-finite-f-divergence",
            "lem-convex-perspective-is-subadditive"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "proof_obligations": [
            "Apply perspective subadditivity at each output symbol.",
            "Use positive homogeneity and the row-sum-one kernel identity.",
            "Retain singular support terms."
          ],
          "statement": "Let $P,Q$ be probability vectors on a finite nonempty alphabet $\\mathcal X$,\nlet $K$ be a stochastic kernel from $\\mathcal X$ to a finite nonempty\nalphabet $\\mathcal Y$, and let $f$ be a convex generator as in\n[[def-finite-f-divergence]]. Then\n$$D_f(PK\\|QK)\\le D_f(P\\|Q),$$\nwith both divergences interpreted using the zero and singular-support\nconventions of that definition.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 7, §7.2, Theorems 7.3–7.4",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-2022.pdf"
              },
