          "strategy": "Prove ln t <= t-1 by its derivative, then sum over the support of p; strict positivity of q handles the zero coordinates of p."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-base-two-entropy-of-a-finite-source",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Base-two entropy of a finite source",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-discrete-source-and-blocks",
            "def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices",
            "def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": [],
          "statement": "For a finite source $p$ of [[def-finite-discrete-source-and-blocks]], define its **base-two Shannon entropy**, measured in bits, by\n$$H_2(p):=\\frac{H(p)}{\\ln 2}=-\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i\\log_2 p_i.$$\nHere $H(p)=-\\sum_i p_i\\ln p_i$ is the finite Shannon entropy in natural units from [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]]. By [[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]], information quantities use natural logarithms by default and $\\log_2 t=\\ln(t)/\\ln 2$. Substituting this identity termwise and using $\\sum_i p_i=1$ gives the displayed equality. Every $p_i$ is positive by the source convention, so no zero-term convention is needed here.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
