        {
          "id": "def-binary-source-code-and-extension",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite binary source codes, extension, and expected length",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-discrete-source-and-blocks"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": [],
          "statement": "A **binary word** is a finite sequence of symbols from $\\{0,1\\}$. The set $\\{0,1\\}^*$ includes the empty word $\\varepsilon$, and $\\{0,1\\}^+$ is the set of nonempty binary words. A **binary source code** for the finite source $\\mathcal X=[m]$ is a function $C:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}^+$. Its codeword length is $\\ell_i:=|C(i)|\\ge1$.\n\nThe **extension** $C^*:\\mathcal X^*\\to\\{0,1\\}^*$ maps the empty source word to $\\varepsilon$ and maps $i_1\\cdots i_n$ to $C(i_1)\\cdots C(i_n)$. The code is **uniquely decodable** if $C^*$ is injective, including comparisons between source words of different lengths. Its expected length for source law $p$ is\n$$L_p(C):=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i\\ell_i.$$",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Stanford EE376A, Information Theory Course Notes, Chapter 4: Lossless Compression",
