                "locator": "§4.1, definitions of uniquely decodable and prefix codes"
              },
              {
                "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 6.450, Memory-less sources, prefix free codes, and entropy",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/f57623d4056984d18685fdb37cea01d6_MIT6_450F09_slide03.pdf",
                "locator": "PDF slides 1–2, finite source symbols and integer binary codeword lengths"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "Define nonempty binary words, code extension, unique decodability, and expected length."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-binary-prefix-code",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Binary prefix codes and their code trees",
          "deps": [
            "def-binary-source-code-and-extension"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [],
          "statement": "A binary code $C$ is a **prefix code** if it is nonsingular and no codeword $C(i)$ is a proper prefix of a different codeword $C(j)$. A word $u$ is a proper prefix of $v$ when $v=uw$ for some nonempty binary word $w$. Nonsingular means $C(i)\\ne C(j)$ whenever $i\\ne j$.\n\nThe **code tree** is the finite rooted trie whose vertices are all prefixes of codewords, whose root is $\\varepsilon$, and whose edges append one bit, labeled $0$ or $1$. Since the code is prefix-free, its codewords are exactly the leaves; unused branches are absent. A binary word path from the root records its successive edge labels.",
