          ],
          "statement": "For each integer $n\\ge1$, let $(\\mathcal X_n,\\mathcal A_n)$ and $(\\mathcal Y_n,\\mathcal B_n)$ be measurable spaces and let $W_n$ be a probability kernel from the first space to the second ([[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]). The sequence $(W_n)_{n\\ge1}$ is a **channel sequence**, and $n$ is its blocklength. A length-$n$ transmission sends an input in $\\mathcal X_n$ and produces an output with conditional law $W_n$. No consistency between different blocklengths is assumed.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 19 §19.1",
                "url": "https://people.lids.mit.edu/yp/homepage/data/itbook-export.pdf"
              }
