          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-discrete-memoryless-channel-block-kernels-are-stochastic"
          ],
          "proof_obligations": [
            "Specify the fixed finite alphabets and single-use transition kernel.",
            "Define every blocklength kernel by the product formula and state the memoryless convention."
          ],
          "statement": "A **finite discrete memoryless channel (DMC)** is the channel sequence [[def-channel-sequence]] with fixed finite nonempty input and output alphabets $\\mathcal X,\\mathcal Y$ (with every subset measurable) and one stochastic kernel $W(y\\mid x)$ from $\\mathcal X$ to $\\mathcal Y$ ([[def-finite-stochastic-kernel]]). At every blocklength $n\\ge1$ its input and output alphabets are $\\mathcal X^n,\\mathcal Y^n$. For a block word $a^n\\in\\mathcal A^n$, regarded as a function on $\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\}$, use the one-based coordinate labels $a_i:=a^n(i-1)$ for $1\\le i\\le n$. With these labels, its block kernel is\n$$W^n(y^n\\mid x^n):=\\prod_{i=1}^n W(y_i\\mid x_i).$$\nThus the same single-use transition law is applied independently at each coordinate, conditional on the input word. This page imposes no feedback or input-cost constraint.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yury Polyanskiy and Yihong Wu, Information Theory: From Coding to Learning, Chapter 19 §19.1",
