          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jean Gallier, Clifford Algebras, Clifford Groups, and a Generalization of the Quaternions: The Pin and Spin Groups, §1.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0805.0311"
              },
              {
                "title": "M. F. Atiyah, R. Bott, and A. Shapiro, Clifford Modules, Part I, §1",
                "url": "https://www.math.ens.psl.eu/~benoist/refs/ABS.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $k$ be a field of characteristic not $2$, let $V$ be finite-dimensional of dimension $n$, and let $B$ be any symmetric bilinear form on $V$, possibly degenerate; put $Q(v)=B(v,v)$. Fix an ordered basis $e_1,\\ldots,e_n$. Then\n\n$$1,\\qquad \\bar e_{i_1}\\cdots\\bar e_{i_r}\\quad(1\\le i_1<\\cdots<i_r\\le n)$$\n\nis a $k$-basis of $\\mathrm{Cl}(V,Q)$, so $\\dim_k\\mathrm{Cl}(V,Q)=2^n$. Give $\\mathrm{Cl}(V,Q)$ the word-length filtration $F_{-1}=0$ and, for $m\\ge0$, let $F_m$ be the span of products of at most $m$ canonical generators. The natural graded algebra map\n\n$$\\Lambda V\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{gr}_F\\mathrm{Cl}(V,Q)=\\bigoplus_{m\\ge0}F_m/F_{m-1}$$\n\nthat sends $v\\in V$ to the degree-one class of $\\bar v$ is an isomorphism, where the graded product is induced by $F_pF_q\\subseteq F_{p+q}$. No nondegeneracy hypothesis is needed.",
          "status": "draft"
        }
