          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Saunders Mac Lane, Categories for the Working Mathematician, VIII.3",
                "url": "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"
              },
              {
                "title": "Olivier Schiffmann, Lectures on Hall Algebras, arXiv:math/0611617v2, §1.3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0611617v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $W\\le L$ be a subobject in an abelian category, and let\n$q:L\\to L/W$ be the quotient map. Inverse image along $q$ is an order\nisomorphism from the subobjects of $L/W$ to the interval of subobjects\n$U\\le L$ with $W\\le U$. Its inverse sends $U$ to the image $q_*U$,\ncanonically isomorphic to $U/W$. Under this correspondence,\n$$ (L/W)/(U/W)\\cong L/U. $$",
          "source_path": "items/lem-hall-flags-lift-through-a-quotient.md",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "strategy": "Use quotient-map pullbacks to construct the correspondence, identify image subobjects with quotients by kernels, and apply the third isomorphism theorem."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-jordan-quiver-nilpotent-representation-category",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Nilpotent representations of the Jordan quiver",
          "deps": [
            "def-nilpotent-endomorphism",
            "def-jordan-block-and-jordan-string",
            "def-partition-young-diagram-and-conjugate-partition"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Olivier Schiffmann, Lectures on Hall Algebras, arXiv:math/0611617v2, §2.1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0611617v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "The **Jordan quiver** has one vertex and one loop, labelled $t$:\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]\n  \\node[circle,draw,inner sep=2.5pt] (v) at (0,0) {$\\bullet$};\n  \\draw[->] (v) edge[loop above] node {$t$} (v);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```\n\nA finite-dimensional representation over a field $k$ is a pair\n$(V,x)$, where $V$ is a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space and\n$x:V\\to V$ is nilpotent ([[def-nilpotent-endomorphism]]). A morphism\n$(V,x)\\to(V',x')$ is a linear map $f:V\\to V'$ satisfying\n$fx=x'f$, encoded by the commutative square\n\n```tikzcd\nV \\arrow[r, \"x\"] \\arrow[d, \"f\"'] & V \\arrow[d, \"f\"] \\\\\nV' \\arrow[r, \"x'\"'] & V'\n```\n\nFor this page, $\\mathcal J_k$ denotes the small model whose objects are\n$(k^n,X)$, for $n\\ge0$, with $X$ a nilpotent matrix, and whose\nmorphisms are all matrices intertwining the endomorphisms. Every finite-\ndimensional vector space is isomorphic to a standard $k^n$, so the model\nrepresents all finite-dimensional nilpotent representations. For any field\n$k$, write $S=(k,0)$ for the one-dimensional object and\n$I_m=(k^m,J_m(0))$ for the length-$m$ Jordan block, using\n[[def-jordan-block-and-jordan-string]]. For a partition\n$\\lambda=(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_s)$\n([[def-partition-young-diagram-and-conjugate-partition]]), write\n$I_\\lambda$ for the block-diagonal representation with blocks\n$J_{\\lambda_1}(0),\\ldots,J_{\\lambda_s}(0)$.\nUse $S^{\\oplus0}=I_\\varnothing=0$ for the zero object.",
