          "statement": "Assume the inherited Choice hypothesis of the integral/global-basis suppliers. Let $A=(a_{ij})$ be a symmetric generalized Cartan matrix on a finite index set; finite, affine and indefinite type are all allowed. Let $\\nu=\\sqrt{p^a}>1$, $K=\\mathbb Q(\\nu)\\subset\\mathbb R$, and $\\epsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}$. The algebra over $K$ on generators $E_i$ with only the symmetric numerical quantum Serre relations at parameter $\\nu^\\epsilon$ is canonically isomorphic, by $E_i\\mapsto E_i$, to the actual Lusztig integral-half specialization $K\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z[t,t^{-1}],t\\mapsto\\nu^\\epsilon}U_A^+$. For both signs, its finite homogeneous components have the classical PBW ranks and the nondegenerate specialized half form. The Green-form assertion is specifically for $\\epsilon=-1$: on $H_-:=K\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z[t,t^{-1}],t\\mapsto\\nu^{-1}}U_A^-$, relabeling its generators $F_i$ as $E_i$, the primitive-generator coproduct has tensor multiplication\n$$ (x\\otimes y)(x'\\otimes y')=\\nu^{(\\deg y,\\deg x')}xx'\\otimes yy', $$\nand the rescaled form $G(x,y)=(p^a-1)^{-\\operatorname{ht}\\alpha}B_{\\nu^{-1}}(x,y)$ for $x,y\\in H_-[-\\alpha]$, zero in different degrees, has $G(F_i,F_j)=\\delta_{ij}/(p^a-1)$ and satisfies both Hopf adjunctions.",
