          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hiraku Nakajima, Introduction to quiver varieties—for ring and representation theorists, §§4.2, 4.4–4.5",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1611.10000"
              }
            ]
          },
          "source_path": "items/thm-a1-nakajima-hecke-correspondence-gives-sl2-action.md",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "statement": "Use [[def-a1-nakajima-framed-quiver-data]] and\n[[lem-a1-nakajima-varieties-and-hecke-correspondence-are-points]]. Let\n$e_v$ be the fundamental class of $\\mathcal L(v,1)$ in\n$H_0(\\mathcal L(v,1);\\mathbb C)$, for $v=0,1$. Define $F$ by the\n$P_{0,1}$ correspondence only from the $v=0$ summand to the $v=1$ summand,\nand extend it by zero on $v=1$. Define $E$ by the reverse correspondence,\nwith Nakajima's sign $(-1)^{(d(0,1)-d(1,1))/2}$, only from $v=1$ to $v=0$,\nand extend it by zero on $v=0$, where $d(v,1)=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathfrak M(v,1)$.\nThe preceding lemma says both dimensions are zero, so this coefficient is\n$1$. Let $H$ act on the $v$-summand by $1-2v$. Then\n$$E e_0=0,\\quad E e_1=e_0,\\quad F e_0=e_1,\\quad F e_1=0,\\quad H e_0=e_0,\\quad H e_1=-e_1.$$\nThese operators define the standard two-dimensional representation of\n$\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)$, the trace-zero $2\\times2$ matrices with\ncommutator bracket."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-hall-algebra-module",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Modules over a Hall algebra are unital algebra representations",
          "deps": [
            "def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring",
            "def-untwisted-hall-algebra",
            "thm-untwisted-hall-algebra-is-associative-and-unital",
            "def-left-and-right-modules"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Olivier Schiffmann, Lectures on Hall Algebras, arXiv:math/0611617v2, §§2.3–2.4",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0611617v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "source_path": "items/def-hall-algebra-module.md",
          "statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with unit\n([[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]) and let $H$ be a unital associative\n$R$-algebra. An **$R$-linear representation of $H$** on an $R$-module $M$ is\na unital $R$-algebra homomorphism\n$\\rho:H\\to\\operatorname{End}_R(M)$, where $\\operatorname{End}_R(M)$ is the\nring of $R$-linear maps $M\\to M$, with composition as multiplication,\n$\\operatorname{id}_M$ as unit, and scalar map $r\\mapsto r\\operatorname{id}_M$.\nIt defines the unital left action\n$h\\cdot m:=\\rho(h)(m)$; its module axioms are those of\n[[def-left-and-right-modules]]. When $H$ is a Hall algebra, this structure is\ncalled a **Hall-algebra module**.\n\nThis term does not mean an object of the input category. An input object $A$\ncontributes a basis symbol $[A]$ to a Hall algebra, but that symbol by itself\nspecifies no representation on an $R$-module. The untwisted Hall algebra\ninstance is supplied by\n[[def-untwisted-hall-algebra]],\n[[thm-untwisted-hall-algebra-is-associative-and-unital]].\n\nThe zero $R$-module is allowed; its identity endomorphism is its zero map.\nThis definition gives an algebra action only. An action on a tensor product\nrequires additional structure, such as a specified coproduct."
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-hall-algebra-regular-module",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "A unital Hall algebra is a left module over itself",
          "deps": [
            "def-hall-algebra-module"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
