          "source_path": "items/thm-jordan-quiver-hall-module-realizes-normalized-heisenberg-fock-action.md",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "statement": "Use the category of finite-dimensional nilpotent Jordan-quiver representations from [[def-jordan-quiver-nilpotent-representation-category]]. Let $t$ be an indeterminate, $K=\\mathbb Q(t)$, and $H_{J,K}:=H_{\\mathrm{gen}}\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z[t]}K$ be the generic Jordan-quiver Hall algebra extended to $K$. Extend the exact isomorphism\n$\\Phi_t$ of [[thm-generic-jordan-hall-algebra-is-symmetric-functions]] to\n$K$; its regular module is identified with\n$\\Lambda_K:=K\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\Lambda=K[p_1,p_2,\\ldots]$. For a partition\n$\\lambda$, let $m_r(\\lambda)$ be the multiplicity of $r$, set\n$p_\\lambda=\\prod_{r\\ge1}p_r^{m_r(\\lambda)}$ and\n$z_\\lambda=\\prod_{r\\ge1}r^{m_r(\\lambda)}m_r(\\lambda)!$, and put\n$p_\\varnothing=1$. Write $\\lambda\\setminus(n)$ for the partition obtained\nby removing one part $n$ when $m_n(\\lambda)>0$; define\n$m_n(\\lambda)p_{\\lambda\\setminus(n)}=0$ when $m_n(\\lambda)=0$. Transport\nthe Jordan Green pairing of\n[[thm-jordan-hall-pairing-and-hall-littlewood-basis]] to $\\Lambda_K$. On\npower sums it is\n$$ (p_\\lambda,p_\\mu)_G=\\delta_{\\lambda\\mu}z_\\lambda\\prod_i(t^{\\lambda_i}-1)^{-1}, $$\nwhere $\\delta_{\\lambda\\mu}=1$ if $\\lambda=\\mu$ and $0$ otherwise, and the\nproduct runs over the parts of $\\lambda$ with multiplicity. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$X_n$ be multiplication by $p_n$ and define\n$$ X_n^{\\dagger,G}p_\\lambda=\\frac{n\\,m_n(\\lambda)}{t^n-1}p_{\\lambda\\setminus(n)},\\qquad P_n:=\\frac{t^n-1}{n}X_n^{\\dagger,G}. $$\nThen $X_n^{\\dagger,G}$ is the adjoint of $X_n$ for $(\\ ,\\ )_G$,\n$P_n=\\partial/\\partial p_n$, and for each finite $N\\ge1$ the assignments\n$X_i\\mapsto (p_i\\text{-multiplication})$, $P_i\\mapsto\\partial/\\partial p_i$,\nand $Z\\mapsto\\operatorname{id}$ give a representation of the finite\nHeisenberg Lie algebra $\\mathfrak h_N(K)$ from\n[[def-heisenberg-lie-algebra]]. The constant $1$ is the vacuum: $P_n1=0$,\nand the vectors $\\prod_{i=1}^N X_i^{m_i}1$, with nonnegative integer\nexponents, span the subalgebra $K[p_1,\\ldots,p_N]$; as $N$ varies, they span\n$\\Lambda_K$."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-a1-arbitrary-framing-cotangent-hecke-model",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Arbitrary-framing A1 varieties and their conormal Hecke correspondence",
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "def-a1-nakajima-framed-quiver-data",
            "def-grassmannian-subspaces",
            "lem-grassmannian-standard-affine-charts",
            "cor-grassmannian-smooth-irreducible-dimension",
            "thm-projective-bundle-represents-line-quotients"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix $W=\\mathbb C^w$, $w\\ge0$, and the no-arrow\none-vertex quiver. For $0\\le v\\le w$, stable data are\n$I:W\\to V$, $J:V\\to W$, $\\dim V=v$, with $IJ=0$ and $J$ injective;\n$g\\in\\operatorname{GL}(V)$ acts by $(gI,Jg^{-1})$. This extends the\n$w=1$ convention in [[def-a1-nakajima-framed-quiver-data]]. The algebraic\norbit space is\n$$M_v=T^*X_v=\\{(S,A):S\\in X_v=\\operatorname{Gr}(v,W),\\ \\operatorname{im}A\\subseteq S\\subseteq\\ker A\\},$$\nunder $S=\\operatorname{im}J$, $A=JI$. Put $a_v=v(w-v)$, so\n$\\dim X_v=a_v$, $\\dim M_v=2a_v$. The map\n$\\mu_v:M_v\\to\\operatorname{End}(W)$, $(S,A)\\mapsto A$, is projective;\nits zero fiber $L_v=X_v$ is the zero section.\n\nFor $0\\le v<w$, put $Y_v=\\operatorname{Fl}(v,v+1;W)$ and write\n$q:Y_v\\to X_v$, $r:Y_v\\to X_{v+1}$. The framed subdatum Hecke locus is\n$$Z_v=\\{(S\\subset T,A):\\operatorname{im}A\\subseteq S,\\ T\\subseteq\\ker A\\} =\\operatorname{Tot}\\mathcal K_v,\\qquad \\mathcal K_v=\\operatorname{Hom}(W/\\mathcal T,\\mathcal S).$$\nIts image in $M_v\\times M_{v+1}$ uses the same $A$ in both factors.\nIt is the conormal bundle to $Y_v\\subset X_v\\times X_{v+1}$ after applying\nfiberwise negation in the second cotangent factor. Both projections are\nprojective, $\\dim Z_v=a_v+a_{v+1}$, and\n$Z_v\\cap\\operatorname{pr}_1^{-1}(L_v)=Y_v\n=Z_v\\cap\\operatorname{pr}_2^{-1}(L_{v+1})$ scheme-theoretically.\nAll these constructions are $\\operatorname{GL}(W)$-equivariant.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "forward_refs": [],
          "dependency_level": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-a1-hecke-zero-section-excess-coefficients",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Excess intersection computes the A1 Hecke coefficients",
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "lem-a1-arbitrary-framing-cotangent-hecke-model",
            "def-refined-gysin-pullback-for-regular-embeddings",
            "lem-zero-section-gysin-and-excess-vector-subbundle",
            "lem-operational-chern-classes-and-whitney-formula",
            "thm-projective-bundle-formula-for-chow-groups"
          ],
          "statement": "Use $X_v,M_v,L_v,Y_v,Z_v,q,r,\\mathcal K_v$ and $a_v$ from\n[[lem-a1-arbitrary-framing-cotangent-hecke-model]]. All Chow groups have\nrational coefficients. Intersect the fundamental cycle $[Z_v]$ with the\nsource zero section $L_v\\times M_{v+1}\\hookrightarrow M_v\\times M_{v+1}$\nby its refined Gysin map, keeping support on $Y_v$, and push along $r$.\nThe result is\n$$r_*\\bigl(c_v(\\Omega_{Y_v/X_{v+1}})\\cap[Y_v]\\bigr) =(-1)^v(v+1)[X_{v+1}]\\in A_{a_{v+1}}(L_{v+1})_{\\mathbb Q}.$$\nFor the reversed correspondence, intersection with the other zero section\nand push along $q$ gives\n$$q_*\\bigl(c_{w-v-1}(\\Omega_{Y_v/X_v})\\cap[Y_v]\\bigr) =(-1)^{w-v-1}(w-v)[X_v]\\in A_{a_v}(L_v)_{\\mathbb Q}.$$\nThese are supported intersection calculations; they do not replace supported\nclasses by their potentially noninjective pushforward into $A_*(M_v)$.\nThe excess and Euler sequences used below are equivariant for\n$\\operatorname{GL}(W)$, although the asserted coefficient formulas are in\nordinary Chow groups.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "forward_refs": [],
          "dependency_level": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-a1-supported-chow-convolution-and-action",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Supported Chow convolution and its action for the A1 family",
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "lem-a1-arbitrary-framing-cotangent-hecke-model",
            "def-refined-gysin-pullback-for-regular-embeddings",
            "lem-refined-gysin-commutation-and-composition",
            "lem-proper-pushforward-of-cycles-well-defined"
          ],
          "statement": "For $0\\le u,v\\le w$ use the smooth $M_v$ and projective maps $\\mu_v$ of\n[[lem-a1-arbitrary-framing-cotangent-hecke-model]], and put\n$R_{uv}=M_u\\times_{\\operatorname{End}(W)}M_v$. A cycle on $R_{uv}$ is\nregarded as a cycle with this support in the smooth product. Define\n$$D\\star C=(p_{13})_*\\Delta_{M_v}^!(C\\times D), \\qquad C\\in A_c(R_{uv})_{\\mathbb Q},\\quad D\\in A_d(R_{vt})_{\\mathbb Q},$$\nwhere the diagonal identifies the two middle factors. Its degree is\n$c+d-2a_v$ and its support is in $R_{ut}$. For\n$\\alpha\\in A_k(L_u)_{\\mathbb Q}$ define\n$$C\\star\\alpha=(p_v)_*\\Delta_{M_u}^!(\\alpha\\times C) \\in A_{k+c-2a_u}(L_v)_{\\mathbb Q}.$$\nAll pushforwards here are proper on the indicated supports. Convolution is\nassociative, the diagonal fundamental classes are units, and\n$D\\star(C\\star\\alpha)=(D\\star C)\\star\\alpha$.\nA cycle of degree $a_u+a_v$ therefore acts between the top zero-fiber\nChow groups. For $\\alpha=[L_u]$, its action is equivalently the refined\nintersection of $C$ with $L_u\\times M_v\\hookrightarrow M_u\\times M_v$,\nfollowed by pushforward with support to $L_v$.\nThese operations commute with the $\\operatorname{GL}(W)$-actions on\nordinary Chow groups.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "forward_refs": [],
          "dependency_level": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-a1-arbitrary-framing-geometric-sl2-action",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Arbitrary-framing A1 Hecke cycles realize the highest-weight sl2 module",
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "lem-a1-arbitrary-framing-cotangent-hecke-model",
            "lem-a1-hecke-zero-section-excess-coefficients",
            "lem-a1-supported-chow-convolution-and-action"
          ],
          "statement": "For $W=\\mathbb C^w$, $w\\ge0$, and the no-arrow one-vertex quiver with\nmoment equation $IJ=0$ and stability $J$ injective, let\n$$\\mathcal V_w=\\bigoplus_{v=0}^w A_{a_v}(L_v)_{\\mathbb Q},\\qquad b_v=[L_v],\\qquad a_v=v(w-v).$$\nUse the algebraic quotient and conormal Hecke cycles $Z_v$ of\n[[lem-a1-arbitrary-framing-cotangent-hecke-model]] and the supported\nconvolution action of [[lem-a1-supported-chow-convolution-and-action]].\nDefine $F$ on the $v$-summand by $[Z_v]$ for $v<w$ and zero at $v=w$.\nDefine $E$ from the $(v+1)$-summand by the reversed cycle with coefficient\n$(-1)^{a_v-a_{v+1}}=(-1)^{w-1}$, and zero at $v=0$.\nLet $H$ act by $w-2v$ on the $v$-summand. Then\n$$F b_v=(-1)^v(v+1)b_{v+1},\\qquad E b_{v+1}=(-1)^v(w-v)b_v,\\qquad H b_v=(w-2v)b_v.$$\nThe nonexistent endpoint terms are zero. These geometrically defined\noperators satisfy $[E,F]=H$, $[H,E]=2E$, $[H,F]=-2F$ and give the\nirreducible integrable highest-weight-$w$ representation of\n$\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb Q)$. Scalar extension gives the corresponding\ncomplex representation. All geometric maps and the action on ordinary\nChow groups are $\\operatorname{GL}(W)$-equivariant. This theorem concerns\nordinary top zero-fiber Chow groups; it does not assert an action on the\nentire equivariant Chow groups with coefficient parameters retained.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "forward_refs": [],
          "dependency_level": 3
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-physics-algebras-track.md",
      "order": 2920
    }

