      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-motivic-stack-function-groups-for-quiver-representations",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Relative motivic stack-function groups for quiver representation stacks",
          "deps": [
            "def-algebraic-stack-and-inertia",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Dominic Joyce, Configurations in abelian categories. II. Ringel-Hall algebras, arXiv:math/0503029v2, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0503029v4"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tom Bridgeland, An Introduction to Motivic Hall Algebras, arXiv:1002.4372v1, §§2.1–3.5",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1002.4372v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Fix a field $k$. An Artin $k$-stack is as in\n[[def-algebraic-stack-and-inertia]]. In this item, a finite-type stack means an\nArtin $k$-stack with a finite-type smooth atlas and finite-type diagonal. It\nhas affine stabilizers when the automorphism group scheme of every geometric\npoint is affine.\n\nFor a finite-type Artin stack $X$ with affine stabilizers, define\n$\\mathscr K(X)$ to be the abelian group generated by isomorphism classes\n$[Y\\to X]$ of finite-type Artin stacks $Y$ with affine stabilizers and a\n1-morphism to $X$. Impose the following relations.\n\n1. If $Z\\hookrightarrow Y$ is a closed substack, then\n   $[Y\\to X]=[Z\\to X]+[Y\\setminus Z\\to X]$.\n2. Let $S\\to X$ be a finite-type stack with affine stabilizers and let\n   $d:V_0\\to V_1$ be a constant-rank morphism of vector bundles on $S$.\n   The additive vector bundle $V_0$ acts on $V_1$ by translation through\n   $d$. Write $[V_1/V_0\\to S\\to X]$ for the resulting quotient groupoid\n   stack over $X$. If $r_i$ is the rank of $V_i$, impose\n   $[V_1/V_0\\to X]=\\mathbb L^{r_1-r_0}[S\\to X]$.\n\nHere $\\mathbb L$ is the class of $\\mathbb A^1_k$ in\n$R:=\\mathscr K(\\operatorname{Spec}k)$, and we localize $R$ and the modules\n$\\mathscr K(X)$ by making $\\mathbb L$ invertible. The product in $R$ is\ninduced by product over $k$. The group $\\mathscr K(X)$ is an $R$-module.\nThis explicit vector-bundle-stack relation records the class of a family of\nlinear objects modulo its linear changes of splitting.\n\nFor a morphism $f:X\\to X'$, pullback is\n$f^*[Y\\to X']=[Y\\times_{X'}X\\to X]$ and pushforward is\n$f_![Z\\to X]=[Z\\to X\\to X']$. Exterior product is\n$[Y\\to X]\\boxtimes[Z\\to X']=[Y\\times_k Z\\to X\\times_kX']$.\nThese operations are well-defined because fiber product and composition\npreserve isomorphisms, closed-substack decompositions, and the\nvector-bundle-stack relation. If the rank of a vector-bundle map varies, its\nbase is partitioned into the finitely many locally closed rank strata and\nthe scissor relation is applied on those strata.\n\nThe dependency on [[def-axiom-of-choice]] is inherited from the cited\nalgebraic-stack convention in [[def-algebraic-stack-and-inertia]], specifically\nits represented-sheaf descent foundation. No choice is used in the\nfinite-dimensional quiver matrix calculations below.",
          "source_path": "items/def-motivic-stack-function-groups-for-quiver-representations.md"
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-derived-and-cohomological-hall-are-distinct",
          "kind": "remark",
          "title": "Derived and cohomological Hall algebras are distinct constructions",
          "deps": [
            "def-motivic-hall-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver",
            "def-toen-derived-hall-algebra",
            "def-toen-locally-finite-dg-category",
            "def-locally-finite-homotopy-type",
            "def-untwisted-quiver-cohomological-hall-algebra",
            "def-equivariant-borel-moore-homology-and-correspondence-convolution",
            "def-quiver-potential-and-monodromic-vanishing-cycle-data",
            "def-critical-quiver-coha-from-vanishing-cycle-data",
            "def-intrinsic-quotient-stack-borel-moore-grading",
            "thm-quiver-quotient-stack-borel-moore-operation-package",
            "lem-standard-rational-quiver-vanishing-cycles-realize-critical-correspondences"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Bertrand Toën, Derived Hall Algebras, arXiv:math/0501343v4, §§3–4",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0501343v4"
              },
              {
                "title": "Maxim Kontsevich and Yan Soibelman, Cohomological Hall algebra, exponential Hodge structures and motivic Donaldson–Thomas invariants, arXiv:1006.2706v2, §§2 and 8",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1006.2706v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "The present page uses the ordinary finite-quiver representation stacks and\nrelative Grothendieck stack-function classes of\n[[def-motivic-hall-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver]]. Toën's derived Hall\nconstruction is defined on the core homotopy type of perfect modules over a\nlocally finite dg-enhancement over a finite field\n([[def-toen-derived-hall-algebra]],\n[[def-toen-locally-finite-dg-category]]). Its arrows are weighted by homotopy\ncardinality after fixing source and homotopy cofiber; the locally finite\nhomotopy-type definition specifies the finite higher-homotopy weights\n([[def-locally-finite-homotopy-type]]). This is a different coefficient\ncarrier from Grothendieck classes of ordinary representation stacks.\n\nThe untwisted quiver CoHA uses rational equivariant\nBorel–Moore homology of representation spaces and the exact-sequence\ncorrespondence ([[def-untwisted-quiver-cohomological-hall-algebra]],\n[[def-equivariant-borel-moore-homology-and-correspondence-convolution]]).\nIts convention is subrepresentation first and quotient second. It uses the intrinsic quotient-stack grading\n$\\mathbb H_i([X/G])=H_{i+2\\dim G}^{\\mathrm{BM},G}(X)$ of\n[[def-intrinsic-quotient-stack-borel-moore-grading]], rather than the variety grading of the published fixed-group definition. Its\nhomological shift is $-2\\chi_Q(\\mathbf b,\\mathbf a)$ for subdimension $\\mathbf a$\nand quotient dimension $\\mathbf b$. The CoHA definition constructs that product through the locally proved parabolic/Levi quotient-stack operation package; its intrinsic shift must not be assigned to the raw variety-normalized groups. For a quiver potential, the critical construction retains monodromy and uses coherent homological coefficients\n([[def-quiver-potential-and-monodromic-vanishing-cycle-data]],\n[[def-critical-quiver-coha-from-vanishing-cycle-data]]). The standard rational vanishing-cycle instance is constructed in\n[[lem-standard-rational-quiver-vanishing-cycles-realize-critical-correspondences]], using the quotient-stack maps of\n[[thm-quiver-quotient-stack-borel-moore-operation-package]]. Its coefficient is the variety-normalized dual shifted by $-2\\dim G$, so the same intrinsic Euler-form degree applies. This does not assert a mixed Hodge enrichment or a general 3-Calabi–Yau orientation-data theorem.\n\nThese constructions have different carriers, coefficient theories, and\nconventions; a shared extension pattern does not identify their algebras. No\ncomparison map between them, orientation-dependent 3-Calabi–Yau integration\ntheorem, or physical BPS state identification is claimed here.",
          "source_path": "items/rem-derived-and-cohomological-hall-are-distinct.md"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-motivic-hall-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "The motivic Hall algebra of a finite quiver",
          "deps": [
            "def-motivic-stack-function-groups-for-quiver-representations"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Dominic Joyce, Configurations in abelian categories. II. Ringel-Hall algebras, arXiv:math/0503029v2, §5.1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0503029v4"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tom Bridgeland, An Introduction to Motivic Hall Algebras, arXiv:1002.4372v1, §§4.1–4.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1002.4372v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $Q=(Q_0,Q_1,s,t)$ be a finite quiver over a field $k$. A representation\nof $Q$ assigns a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space $V_i$ to each vertex\n$i\\in Q_0$ and a linear map $V_{s(a)}\\to V_{t(a)}$ to each arrow\n$a\\in Q_1$. Its dimension vector is\n$\\mathbf d=(\\dim V_i)_{i\\in Q_0}\\in\\mathbb N^{Q_0}$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf d\\in\\mathbb N^{Q_0}$ put\n$$\\operatorname{Rep}_{\\mathbf d}(Q) =\\prod_{a\\in Q_1}\\operatorname{Hom}_k (k^{d_{s(a)}},k^{d_{t(a)}}),\\qquad G_{\\mathbf d}=\\prod_{i\\in Q_0}\\operatorname{GL}_{d_i}(k),$$and let $\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)=[\\operatorname{Rep}_{\\mathbf d}(Q)/ G_{\\mathbf d}]$ be the quotient Artin stack. It is finite type and has affine stabilizers: the representation variety and the acting group are finite type and affine, and each stabilizer is a closed subgroup of $G_{\\mathbf d}$. For dimension vectors $\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e$, let $\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}(Q)$ be the stack of short exact sequences of representations$$0\\longrightarrow B_{\\mathbf e}\\longrightarrow E_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e} \\longrightarrow A_{\\mathbf d}\\longrightarrow0.$$The endpoints are part of the data, and isomorphisms are isomorphisms of the whole exact sequence. Equivalently, after choosing the standard $\\mathbf e$-dimensional subspaces at each vertex, this is the quotient by the product parabolic preserving those subspaces of the affine space of block upper-triangular arrow maps. Thus $\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}(Q)$ is a finite-type Artin stack with affine stabilizers. More explicitly, on a local splitting $E_i=B_i\\oplus A_i$, the arrow $a:i\\to j$ has block matrix$$\\begin{pmatrix} B_a & \\xi_a\\\\ 0 & A_a \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\xi_a:A_i\\to B_j.$$Changing the splitting by $h_i:A_i\\to B_i$ changes $\\xi_a$ to $\\xi_a+B_a h_i-h_j A_a$. This is the translation action of the vector bundle $\\bigoplus_i\\operatorname{Hom}(A_i,B_i)$ on $\\bigoplus_{a:i\\to j}\\operatorname{Hom}(A_i,B_j)$ used in the extension stack calculation. Let $q:\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}\\to\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}$ send a sequence to its quotient, $s:\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}\\to \\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf e}$ send it to its subrepresentation, and $m:\\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}\\to\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e}$ send it to its middle representation. The Hall correspondence is\n\n~~~tikzcd\n& \\mathfrak E_{\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e}(Q) \\ar[dl,\"(q,s)\"'] \\ar[dr,\"m\"] & \\\\ \\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)\\times\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf e}(Q) && \\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e}(Q)\n~~~\n\nIts arrows record the ordered quotient, subrepresentation, and middle object of the displayed short exact sequence; the diagram fixes the convolution convention. Define$$H_Q:=\\bigoplus_{\\mathbf d\\in\\mathbb N^{Q_0}} \\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)).$$For $\\alpha\\in\\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d})$ and $\\beta\\in\\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf e})$, define$$\\alpha*\\beta :=m_!\\big((q,s)^*(\\alpha\\boxtimes\\beta)\\big) \\in\\mathscr K(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d+\\mathbf e}).$$\nThus the first factor is the quotient and the second factor is the\nsubrepresentation, so the sequence is\n$0\\to B\\to E\\to A\\to0$. Each homogeneous product is defined by one\nfinite-type stack correspondence; no completion or finiteness of a sum over\nisomorphism classes is required. This is the stack-function product without\na potential or a vanishing-cycle weight.",
          "source_path": "items/def-motivic-hall-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver.md"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-finite-field-stack-groupoid-cardinality",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite-field groupoid cardinality for quiver stack specializations",
          "deps": [
            "def-motivic-stack-function-groups-for-quiver-representations"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-finite-field-stack-groupoid-cardinality-respects-motivic-relations"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Dominic Joyce, Configurations in abelian categories. II. Ringel-Hall algebras, arXiv:math/0503029v2, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0503029v4"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tom Bridgeland, An Introduction to Motivic Hall Algebras, arXiv:1002.4372v1, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1002.4372v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $k=\\mathbb F_q$. A finite groupoid is a groupoid with finitely many\nisomorphism classes of objects and finite automorphism groups. Its\n**groupoid cardinality** is\n$$|\\mathcal G|=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\operatorname{Iso}(\\mathcal G)} \\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal G}(x)|}.$$\nFor a quotient stack in the finite-quiver construction whose rational-point\ngroupoid is finite, define its point-count value by the groupoid cardinality\nof that rational-point groupoid. This definition applies, in particular, to\n$\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)$, the exact-sequence stacks, and the\nvector-bundle-stack fibers in the examples below. Its compatibility with the\nrelations defining $\\mathscr K$ is established by\n[[lem-finite-field-stack-groupoid-cardinality-respects-motivic-relations]].",
          "source_path": "items/def-finite-field-stack-groupoid-cardinality.md"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-quiver-motivic-extension-stack-class",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The motive of a quiver extension stack",
          "deps": [
            "def-motivic-stack-function-groups-for-quiver-representations",
            "def-motivic-hall-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver",
            "def-algebraic-stack-and-inertia",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Tom Bridgeland, An Introduction to Motivic Hall Algebras, arXiv:1002.4372v1, §4.1 and §7.1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1002.4372v1"
              },
              {
                "title": "Dominic Joyce, Configurations in abelian categories. II. Ringel-Hall algebras, arXiv:math/0503029v2, §§4–5",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0503029v4"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $Q$ be a finite quiver over $k$, and let $X\\to\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf d}(Q)$ and\n$Y\\to\\mathfrak M_{\\mathbf e}(Q)$ be finite-type stack families with affine\nstabilizers of quotient representations $A$ and subrepresentations $B$. Let\n$\\mathfrak E_{X,Y}$ be the stack of exact sequences\n$0\\to B\\to E\\to A\\to0$ with the endpoint identifications supplied by $X$\nand $Y$. Define the bilinear Euler form\n$$\\chi_Q(\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e) =\\sum_{i\\in Q_0}d_i e_i-\\sum_{a\\in Q_1}d_{s(a)}e_{t(a)}.$$Then the relative motivic class over $X\\times Y$ is$$[\\mathfrak E_{X,Y}\\to X\\times Y] =\\mathbb L^{-\\chi_Q(\\mathbf d,\\mathbf e)} [X\\times Y\\to X\\times Y]$$\nin the vector-bundle-stack coefficient theory of\n[[def-motivic-stack-function-groups-for-quiver-representations]]. The\nstatement concerns extension groupoids, so automorphisms of an extension\nthat act trivially on its two endpoints are retained.",
          "source_path": "items/lem-quiver-motivic-extension-stack-class.md",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "strategy": "Represent extensions by off-diagonal arrow maps modulo changes of splitting and apply the vector-stack relation on finite rank strata."
        },
        {
