          "statement": "Assume countable choice. Let $\\mathcal C$ be a ribbon category with chosen left duals. Consider finite oriented framed ribbon graphs with boundary collars, bands colored by arbitrary objects of $\\mathcal C$, and coupons colored by arbitrary morphisms between the tensor products of their incident signed objects. There is a unique monoidal evaluation assigning each coupon its declared morphism, crossings the braiding or its correctly typed inverse, cups/caps the chosen duality maps and their ribbon-induced opposite variants, and full twists $\\theta^{\\pm1}$. It is invariant under every boundary-relative framed graph isotopy and preserves composition, tensor product and the empty graph. In a strict model it is strict monoidal; transport gives the corresponding strong monoidal evaluation in $\\mathcal C$.\n\nMore precisely, for a signed list $A=(Y_1^{\\epsilon_1},\\ldots,Y_p^{\\epsilon_p})$, put $|A|=\\bigotimes_sY_s^{\\epsilon_s}$, with $Y^-=Y^*$. For a coupon $f:|A|\\to|B|$, its strand passages and full rotation are the complete composites\n\n$$c_{|B|,Z}(f\\otimes1_Z)=(1_Z\\otimes f)c_{|A|,Z},\\qquad c_{Z,|B|}(1_Z\\otimes f)=(f\\otimes1_Z)c_{Z,|A|},\\qquad\\theta_{|B|}f\\theta_{|A|}^{-1}=f.$$\n\nTheir tensor-list expansions, all inverse-crossing versions and the explicit dual-mate formulas supply every coupon move; the claim includes zero-input/output coupons and all band orientations. No assumption that all colors are tensor powers of one object is imposed.",
