          "statement": "Assume AC, carried through Poincaré–Lefschetz duality. Use outward-normal-first boundary orientations and the Walker Maslov form: on triples $a_1+a_2+a_3=0$, $a_i\\in L_i$, it is $\\psi(a,a')=\\omega(a_1,a_2')$, with signature $\\mu_W(L_1,L_2,L_3)$ after removing the radical.\n\nIf an oriented four-manifold is decomposed as $W=W_1\\cup_{M_0}W_2$, with\n\n$$\\partial W_1=M_0\\cup(-M_1),\\qquad\\partial W_2=(-M_0)\\cup M_2,\\qquad\\Sigma=\\partial M_0=\\partial M_1=\\partial M_2,$$\n\nput $K_i=\\ker[H_1(\\Sigma;\\mathbb R)\\to H_1(M_i;\\mathbb R)]$. Then\n\n$$\\sigma(W)=\\sigma(W_1)+\\sigma(W_2)-\\mu_W(K_1,K_0,K_2).$$\n\nFor the actual standard-cap special-graph stacking presentations of [[lem-rt-capped-operator-normalization-and-composition]], let $\\alpha_q$ be the meridian Lagrangians of the chosen reference caps and $R_q$ the individual bordism homology relations. With the canonical middle-object orientation, the actual surgery signatures satisfy\n\n$$\\Delta\\sigma:=\\sigma(B_{\\rm comp})-\\sigma(B_1)-\\sigma(B_2)=\\mu_W(R_1\\alpha_0,\\alpha_1,R_2^t\\alpha_2)=:\\mu_\\alpha.$$\n\nFor arbitrary real object Lagrangians $\\lambda_q$, define the actual cap-reference integer\n\n$$a(R;\\lambda,\\alpha)=-\\mu_W(\\lambda_0,\\alpha_0,R^t\\alpha_1)-\\mu_W(R\\lambda_0,\\alpha_1,\\lambda_1).$$\n\nIt satisfies $a(BA)-a(B)-a(A)=\\mu_\\alpha-\\mu_\\lambda$, where $\\mu_\\lambda=\\mu_W(A\\lambda_0,\\lambda_1,B^t\\lambda_2)$. Consequently the correctly weighted operators are\n\n$$Z(M,n;\\lambda)=\\kappa^{n+a(R_M;\\lambda,\\alpha)}Z_\\alpha(M),\\qquad n_{\\rm comp}=n_2+n_1+\\mu_\\lambda.$$\n\nThey compose strictly, preserve the identity and tensor laws, and a unit increase of weight acts by $\\kappa$. In particular the action $\\kappa^{-n}$ is incompatible with this prescribed $+\\mu_W$ composition and the fixed surgery normalization $\\kappa^{-\\sigma(B)}$. These are normalization/signature claims for the indicated presentations; the full relative surgery/Cerf completeness theorem remains separate.",
          "statement_or_definition": "Assume AC, carried through Poincaré–Lefschetz duality. Use outward-normal-first boundary orientations and the Walker Maslov form: on triples $a_1+a_2+a_3=0$, $a_i\\in L_i$, it is $\\psi(a,a')=\\omega(a_1,a_2')$, with signature $\\mu_W(L_1,L_2,L_3)$ after removing the radical.\n\nIf an oriented four-manifold is decomposed as $W=W_1\\cup_{M_0}W_2$, with\n\n$$\\partial W_1=M_0\\cup(-M_1),\\qquad\\partial W_2=(-M_0)\\cup M_2,\\qquad\\Sigma=\\partial M_0=\\partial M_1=\\partial M_2,$$\n\nput $K_i=\\ker[H_1(\\Sigma;\\mathbb R)\\to H_1(M_i;\\mathbb R)]$. Then\n\n$$\\sigma(W)=\\sigma(W_1)+\\sigma(W_2)-\\mu_W(K_1,K_0,K_2).$$\n\nFor the actual standard-cap special-graph stacking presentations of [[lem-rt-capped-operator-normalization-and-composition]], let $\\alpha_q$ be the meridian Lagrangians of the chosen reference caps and $R_q$ the individual bordism homology relations. With the canonical middle-object orientation, the actual surgery signatures satisfy\n\n$$\\Delta\\sigma:=\\sigma(B_{\\rm comp})-\\sigma(B_1)-\\sigma(B_2)=\\mu_W(R_1\\alpha_0,\\alpha_1,R_2^t\\alpha_2)=:\\mu_\\alpha.$$\n\nFor arbitrary real object Lagrangians $\\lambda_q$, define the actual cap-reference integer\n\n$$a(R;\\lambda,\\alpha)=-\\mu_W(\\lambda_0,\\alpha_0,R^t\\alpha_1)-\\mu_W(R\\lambda_0,\\alpha_1,\\lambda_1).$$\n\nIt satisfies $a(BA)-a(B)-a(A)=\\mu_\\alpha-\\mu_\\lambda$, where $\\mu_\\lambda=\\mu_W(A\\lambda_0,\\lambda_1,B^t\\lambda_2)$. Consequently the correctly weighted operators are\n\n$$Z(M,n;\\lambda)=\\kappa^{n+a(R_M;\\lambda,\\alpha)}Z_\\alpha(M),\\qquad n_{\\rm comp}=n_2+n_1+\\mu_\\lambda.$$\n\nThey compose strictly, preserve the identity and tensor laws, and a unit increase of weight acts by $\\kappa$. In particular the action $\\kappa^{-n}$ is incompatible with this prescribed $+\\mu_W$ composition and the fixed surgery normalization $\\kappa^{-\\sigma(B)}$. These are normalization/signature claims for the indicated presentations; the full relative surgery/Cerf completeness theorem remains separate.",
