          "statement": "Assume countable choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $Y$ be a compact oriented smooth three-manifold with prescribed boundary parametrizations and a fixed product collar $C\\subset Y$. Surgery links lie in $\\operatorname{Int}(Y\\setminus C)$. For a framed link $L$, let $Y_L$ be its Dehn filling, with the canonical unchanged collar $C$, and let $E_L=Y\\setminus\\operatorname{Int}N(L)$ denote its exterior. The meridian of the $i$th filling solid torus is glued to the surgery-framed parallel $\\lambda_i$ on $\\partial N(L_i)$.\n\nA decoration is a finite properly embedded oriented framed ribbon graph, with fixed abstract coupon set, actual coupon morphisms and band labels, fixed attachment order, and prescribed product ribbons in $C$ meeting only the designated marked boundary intervals. Colored graph isotopies keep all these collars, endpoints and labels fixed. Then:\n\n1. Every decoration in $Y_L$ has a representative in $\\operatorname{Int}(E_L)\\cup C$, relative to its prescribed collar ribbons.\n2. Two such exterior decorations that are isotopic as framed labeled graphs in $Y_L$ are connected by finitely many exterior framed colored graph isotopies and framed slides of open graph strands over the surgery parallels $\\lambda_i$. Original coupons and colors remain fixed.\n3. Suppose the **marked undecorated relative surgery calculus** holds in $Y$: every required collared bordism has a marked surgery presentation, and every specified orientation-preserving identification of two outputs, fixed pointwise on $C$, is realized up to isotopy relative to $C$ by a finite sequence of the canonical local undecorated moves $O1$ (a separated $\\pm1$ unknot), $O2$ (a framed surgery-component band slide), $O3$ (a framed knot plus a small zero-framed meridian), and exterior link isotopies. The canonical move maps are the standard local filling identifications, identity outside their specified local neighborhoods. Under exactly this hypothesis, every marked decorated bordism admits an exterior decorated presentation, and two presentations of the same marked decoration are connected by the corresponding undecorated moves, exterior colored graph isotopies, and the open-colored-strand slides in (2).\n\nThe hypothesis in (3) concerns the **specified** output identification, not merely existence of some diffeomorphism. No undecorated completeness theorem is proved or assumed silently here. For the evaluation assertion, take the colors and coupons in a complex modular spherical ribbon fusion category with chosen left duals as in [[def-modular-ribbon-category-and-hopf-link-data]], and put $\\Omega=\\sum_i d_i i$. After surgery components are colored by $\\Omega$, all the extra graph moves in this conclusion preserve the complete fixed-coupon evaluation by [[lem-kirby-color-full-band-slide-and-coupon-identity]]; the usual local $O1/O2/O3$ normalization identities are separate algebraic suppliers. Connectedness is not required.",
          "statement_or_definition": "Assume countable choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $Y$ be a compact oriented smooth three-manifold with prescribed boundary parametrizations and a fixed product collar $C\\subset Y$. Surgery links lie in $\\operatorname{Int}(Y\\setminus C)$. For a framed link $L$, let $Y_L$ be its Dehn filling, with the canonical unchanged collar $C$, and let $E_L=Y\\setminus\\operatorname{Int}N(L)$ denote its exterior. The meridian of the $i$th filling solid torus is glued to the surgery-framed parallel $\\lambda_i$ on $\\partial N(L_i)$.\n\nA decoration is a finite properly embedded oriented framed ribbon graph, with fixed abstract coupon set, actual coupon morphisms and band labels, fixed attachment order, and prescribed product ribbons in $C$ meeting only the designated marked boundary intervals. Colored graph isotopies keep all these collars, endpoints and labels fixed. Then:\n\n1. Every decoration in $Y_L$ has a representative in $\\operatorname{Int}(E_L)\\cup C$, relative to its prescribed collar ribbons.\n2. Two such exterior decorations that are isotopic as framed labeled graphs in $Y_L$ are connected by finitely many exterior framed colored graph isotopies and framed slides of open graph strands over the surgery parallels $\\lambda_i$. Original coupons and colors remain fixed.\n3. Suppose the **marked undecorated relative surgery calculus** holds in $Y$: every required collared bordism has a marked surgery presentation, and every specified orientation-preserving identification of two outputs, fixed pointwise on $C$, is realized up to isotopy relative to $C$ by a finite sequence of the canonical local undecorated moves $O1$ (a separated $\\pm1$ unknot), $O2$ (a framed surgery-component band slide), $O3$ (a framed knot plus a small zero-framed meridian), and exterior link isotopies. The canonical move maps are the standard local filling identifications, identity outside their specified local neighborhoods. Under exactly this hypothesis, every marked decorated bordism admits an exterior decorated presentation, and two presentations of the same marked decoration are connected by the corresponding undecorated moves, exterior colored graph isotopies, and the open-colored-strand slides in (2).\n\nThe hypothesis in (3) concerns the **specified** output identification, not merely existence of some diffeomorphism. No undecorated completeness theorem is proved or assumed silently here. For the evaluation assertion, take the colors and coupons in a complex modular spherical ribbon fusion category with chosen left duals as in [[def-modular-ribbon-category-and-hopf-link-data]], and put $\\Omega=\\sum_i d_i i$. After surgery components are colored by $\\Omega$, all the extra graph moves in this conclusion preserve the complete fixed-coupon evaluation by [[lem-kirby-color-full-band-slide-and-coupon-identity]]; the usual local $O1/O2/O3$ normalization identities are separate algebraic suppliers. Connectedness is not required.",
