      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-finite-semisimple-module-category-over-fusion-category",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite semisimple module category over a fusion category",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-fusion-and-multifusion-category",
            "def-semisimple-object-and-semisimple-abelian-category"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal C$ be a fusion category over $k$. A **finite semisimple right $\\mathcal C$-module category** consists of a $k$-linear abelian category $\\mathcal M$, semisimple in the sense of [[def-semisimple-object-and-semisimple-abelian-category]], with finitely many isomorphism classes of simple objects and finite-dimensional $k$-vector spaces $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal M}(m,n)$ for all $m,n\\in\\mathcal M$, together with a bilinear action functor $\\odot:\\mathcal M\\times\\mathcal C\\to\\mathcal M$ and natural isomorphisms\n$$\\alpha_{m,X,Y}:(m\\odot X)\\odot Y\\longrightarrow m\\odot(X\\otimes Y),\\qquad \\rho_m:m\\odot\\mathbf1\\longrightarrow m,$$\nwhich satisfy the module pentagon and triangle equations obtained from the associator and unitors of $\\mathcal C$. The left-action convention is defined by reversing the order of the action factors.\n\nA **module functor** $F:\\mathcal M\\to\\mathcal N$ is a linear functor with natural action comparisons $F(m\\odot X)\\cong F(m)\\odot X$ satisfying the module associativity and unit equations. A **module-natural transformation** is a natural transformation commuting with those comparisons. These objects, functors, and transformations form a 2-category denoted $\\operatorname{Mod}(\\mathcal C)$ with ordinary composition of module functors and horizontal and vertical composition of module-natural transformations. The action comparison for a composite is the composite of the two action comparisons; identity functors have identity comparisons.\n\nThis definition alone does not assert that there are finitely many equivalence classes of simple module categories, nor that $\\operatorname{Mod}(\\mathcal C)$ is a fusion 2-category.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Douglas and Reutter, Fusion 2-Categories and a State-Sum Invariant for 4-Manifolds, §1.4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.11933"
            },
            {
              "title": "Décoppet, Extension Theory and Fermionic Strongly Fusion 2-Categories, §2.2",
              "url": "https://www.maths.tcd.ie/EMIS/journals/SIGMA/2024/092/sigma24-092.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Décoppet and Yu, Fiber 2-Functors and Tambara-Yamagami Fusion 2-Categories, §2.2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.08117"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "def-g-graded-two-vector-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "G-graded finite semisimple 2-vector space",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-fusion-and-multifusion-category",
            "def-group",
            "def-semisimple-object-and-semisimple-abelian-category",
            "def-simple-object"
          ],
          "statement": "Fix an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic zero and a finite group $G$ ([[def-group]]). A finite semisimple category is understood in the sense of [[def-semisimple-object-and-semisimple-abelian-category]], with simple objects as in [[def-simple-object]]. A **finite semisimple $G$-graded $k$-linear category** is a finite semisimple $k$-linear category $\\mathcal A$ together with a direct-sum decomposition\n$$\\mathcal A=\\bigoplus_{g\\in G}\\mathcal A_g.$$\nAn object in $\\mathcal A_g$ is homogeneous of degree $g$. A grading-preserving linear functor sends each $\\mathcal A_g$ into the corresponding degree-$g$ summand, and its 2-morphisms are natural transformations. Such categories, functors, and natural transformations form the underlying 2-category denoted $2\\mathrm{Vect}_G$.\n\nFor finite semisimple categories $\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B$ with simple-label sets $S,T$, their finite semisimple tensor product $\\mathcal A\\boxtimes\\mathcal B$ has simple labels $S\\times T$ and\n$$\\operatorname{Hom}(s\\boxtimes t,s'\\boxtimes t')=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal A}(s,s')\\otimes_k\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal B}(t,t'),$$\nextended to direct sums. For a concrete skeleton, choose finite sets $S_g$ of simple labels and replace $\\mathcal A_g$ by the category of finite-dimensional $k$-vector-space families indexed by $S_g$. A linear functor is represented by a finite matrix of finite-dimensional vector spaces within each grade. This skeleton fixes the meaning of “finite” and the homogeneous components; it does not assert any physical realization.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Décoppet and Yu, Fiber 2-Functors and Tambara-Yamagami Fusion 2-Categories, §2.1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.08117"
            },
            {
              "title": "Décoppet, Extension Theory and Fermionic Strongly Fusion 2-Categories, Example 2.2",
              "url": "https://www.maths.tcd.ie/EMIS/journals/SIGMA/2024/092/sigma24-092.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "def-modc-weighted-rt-background-interface",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Weighted RT background interface for Mod(C)",
          "dependency_level": 15,
          "deps": [
            "thm-weighted-rt-tqft-and-factorization"
          ],
          "statement": "",
          "provenance": {
