        {
          "id": "def-separable-algebra-and-bimodule-in-fusion-category",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Separable algebra objects and bimodules in a fusion category",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-fusion-and-multifusion-category"
          ],
          "statement": "In a monoidal category $\\mathcal C$, an **algebra object** is an object $A$ with multiplication $m_A:A\\otimes A\\to A$ and unit $\\eta_A:\\mathbf1\\to A$ satisfying associativity and unit equations with the associator and unitors of $\\mathcal C$. It is **separable** if there is an $A$-bimodule morphism $\\sigma_A:A\\to A\\otimes A$ with $m_A\\sigma_A=1_A$. The composite $e_A=\\sigma_A\\eta_A:\\mathbf1\\to A\\otimes A$ is its **separability element**. It satisfies $m_Ae_A=\\eta_A$ and the centrality equation\n$$ (m_A\\otimes1_A)\\circ(1_A\\otimes e_A)=(1_A\\otimes m_A)\\circ(e_A\\otimes1_A):A\\longrightarrow A\\otimes A,$$\nwith associators inserted. In element notation these are $\\sum_i x_i y_i=1_A$ and $\\sum_i a x_i\\otimes y_i=\\sum_i x_i\\otimes y_i a$.\n\nA **left $A$-module** is an object $M\\in\\mathcal C$ with an associative unital action $l_M:A\\otimes M\\to M$; a **right $A$-module** has an associative unital action $r_M:M\\otimes A\\to M$. Write $\\operatorname{Mod}^{l}_{\\mathcal C}(A)$ and $\\operatorname{Mod}^{r}_{\\mathcal C}(A)$ for their categories and module morphisms. The category of left $A$-modules is a right $\\mathcal C$-module category by $M\\odot X:=M\\otimes X$, with left $A$-action obtained by associating $A\\otimes(M\\otimes X)$ to $(A\\otimes M)\\otimes X$ and applying $l_M\\otimes1_X$, and the inherited associator and unitor. An **$A$-$B$ bimodule** is an object $X\\in\\mathcal C$ with commuting left and right actions $A\\otimes X\\to X$ and $X\\otimes B\\to X$, each associative and unital. A bimodule morphism preserves both actions.\n\nThe **regular $A$-$A$ bimodule** is the object $A$ with both actions given by $m_A$.\n\nGiven an $A$-$B$ bimodule $X$ and a $B$-$D$ bimodule $Y$, a morphism $q:X\\otimes Y\\to Z$ is **balanced over $B$** if\n$$q\\circ(r_X\\otimes1_Y)=q\\circ(1_X\\otimes l_Y):X\\otimes B\\otimes Y\\longrightarrow Z.$$\nA relative tensor product $X\\otimes_B Y$, when it exists, is an object with a balanced map $q_B:X\\otimes Y\\to X\\otimes_B Y$ universal among balanced maps. Existence is not part of this definition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Douglas and Reutter, Fusion 2-Categories and a State-Sum Invariant for 4-Manifolds, §§1.3 and 2.1.3",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.11933"
            },
            {
              "title": "Décoppet and Yu, Fiber 2-Functors and Tambara-Yamagami Fusion 2-Categories, §2.3",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.08117"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "def-weak-linear-two-category",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Weak linear 2-category",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-category",
            "def-functor-and-contravariant-functor",
            "def-natural-transformation",
            "def-strict-two-category"
          ],
          "statement": "Fix a field $k$. A **weak linear 2-category** $\\mathcal B$ consists of a class of objects and, for each ordered pair $(A,B)$, a $k$-linear category $\\mathcal B(A,B)$. Its objects are called **1-morphisms** and its morphisms are called **2-morphisms**. Each triple $A,B,C$ has a $k$-bilinear composition functor\n$$\\mathcal B(B,C)\\times\\mathcal B(A,B)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal B(A,C),\\qquad (g,f)\\longmapsto g\\circ f,$$\nand each $A$ has an identity 1-morphism $1_A\\in\\mathcal B(A,A)$. A $k$-linear category here means that every hom set is a $k$-vector space and composition is bilinear.\n\nFor composable $f:A\\to B$, $g:B\\to C$, and $h:C\\to D$, there is a natural invertible 2-morphism\n$$a_{h,g,f}:(h\\circ g)\\circ f\\Longrightarrow h\\circ(g\\circ f).$$\nFor each $f:A\\to B$ there are natural invertible unit 2-morphisms\n$$\\ell_f:1_B\\circ f\\Longrightarrow f,\\qquad r_f:f\\circ1_A\\Longrightarrow f.$$\nThe associators satisfy the pentagon equation on every fourfold composite; the two unitors and associator satisfy the triangle equation on every pair of composable 1-morphisms. These equations are equalities of 2-morphisms between the indicated parallel 1-morphisms. Vertical composition is composition in each hom category. Horizontal composition is the displayed functor, so functoriality gives the interchange equation between horizontal and vertical composition.\n\nWhen the associators and unitors are identities, this is a strict 2-category in the sense of [[def-strict-two-category]]. No strictness is imposed in the weak definition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Douglas and Reutter, Fusion 2-Categories and a State-Sum Invariant for 4-Manifolds, §§1.1-1.2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.11933"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-canonical-fusion-category-algebra-is-frobenius",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The canonical algebra of a fusion category is Frobenius",
          "deps": [
            "def-fusion-and-multifusion-category"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal C$ be a fusion category over an algebraically closed field. Write $\\mathcal D=\\mathcal C\\boxtimes\\mathcal C^{\\mathrm{rev}}$, acting on $\\mathcal C$ by $(X\\boxtimes Y)\\cdot V=X\\otimes V\\otimes Y$. Choose duals with $\\operatorname{ev}_i:i\\otimes i^*\\to\\mathbf1$ and $\\operatorname{coev}_i:\\mathbf1\\to i^*\\otimes i$. The canonical algebra $K=\\underline{\\operatorname{End}}_{\\mathcal D}(\\mathbf1)$ is $\\bigoplus_i i\\boxtimes i^*$, with multiplication induced by composition of evaluations and unit the inclusion of the unit summand. Projection $\\lambda:K\\to\\mathbf1\\boxtimes\\mathbf1$ to that summand makes $\\beta=\\lambda\\mu$ a nondegenerate pairing. Its inverse $c:\\mathbf1\\to K\\otimes K$ defines a Frobenius comultiplication\n$$\\Delta=(\\mu\\otimes\\mathrm{id})(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes c)=(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes\\mu)(c\\otimes\\mathrm{id}).$$\nThis comultiplication is coassociative, is a $K$-bimodule morphism, and has counit $\\lambda$, with $\\lambda\\eta=1$. No nonvanishing dimension or pivotal structure is assumed.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Etingof, Gelaki, Nikshych, and Ostrik, Tensor Categories, Sections 7.20–7.21",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~etingof/egnobookfinal.pdf"
            }
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-modc-surjective-orbit-tangents-give-finite-orbits",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Surjective orbit tangents give finitely many open orbits",
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "thm-chevalley-constructible-image-varieties",
            "lem-constructible-dense-contains-open",
            "lem-dominant-map-generic-differential-surjectivity-char-zero",
            "lem-classical-variety-noetherian-components",
            "lem-local-dimension-reduced-variety-components",
            "thm-jacobian-criterion-affine-variety",
            "thm-dimension-at-most-embedding-dimension",
            "lem-regular-local-domain-induction",
            "lem-dimension-nonempty-open-subset"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $k$ be algebraically closed of characteristic zero, let $G$ be a finite product of general linear groups, and let $X$ be a reduced affine variety of finite type with an algebraic $G$-action. If the differential of $f_x:G\\to X$, $g\\mapsto gx$, is surjective onto $T_xX$ at the identity for every $x\\in X$, then every orbit is open and there are finitely many orbits. Empty products and the empty variety are allowed.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Etingof, Gelaki, Nikshych, and Ostrik, Tensor Categories, Theorem 9.1.4",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~etingof/egnobookfinal.pdf"
            }
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-separability-contracts-normalized-categorical-hochschild-complex",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "A separability element contracts the normalized categorical Hochschild complex in positive degrees",
          "deps": [
            "def-separable-algebra-and-bimodule-in-fusion-category"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $A$ be an algebra object in a $k$-linear monoidal category and $B$ an $A$-bimodule. Suppose $e:\\mathbf1\\to A\\otimes A$ satisfies\n$$\\mu e=\\eta,\\qquad (\\mu\\otimes\\mathrm{id})(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes e)=(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes\\mu)(e\\otimes\\mathrm{id}).$$\nSet $C^n=\\operatorname{Hom}(A^{\\otimes n},B)$, with the Hochschild differential given by the alternating sum of the left action, adjacent multiplications, and the right action. Its normalized subcomplex consists of maps vanishing whenever any input is the unit $\\eta$. For every $n\\geq1$, define\n$$h_n(f)=\\lambda_B(\\mathrm{id}_A\\otimes f)(e\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_{A^{\\otimes(n-1)}}),$$\nwhere $\\lambda_B$ denotes the left $A$-action. Then $h$ preserves the normalized subcomplex and $dh+hd=\\mathrm{id}$ in every positive degree.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Etingof, Gelaki, Nikshych, and Ostrik, Tensor Categories, Proposition 9.1.1 and formula (9.2)",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~etingof/egnobookfinal.pdf"
            }
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "def-finite-semisimple-two-category",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite semisimple 2-category",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-weak-linear-two-category",
            "def-semisimple-object-and-semisimple-abelian-category",
            "def-monad",
            "def-adjunction-by-unit-counit-and-triangle-identities"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal B$ be a weak linear $2$-category. A **monad on $A$ in $\\mathcal B$** is a $1$-morphism $T:A\\to A$ with $2$-morphisms $\\eta:1_A\\Rightarrow T$ and $\\mu:T\\circ T\\Rightarrow T$ satisfying\n$$\\mu\\cdot(\\mu\\ast1_T)=\\mu\\cdot(1_T\\ast\\mu),\\qquad \\mu\\cdot(\\eta\\ast1_T)=1_T=\\mu\\cdot(1_T\\ast\\eta),$$\nwhere $\\cdot$ is vertical composition, $\\ast$ is horizontal composition, and the associator and unitors of $\\mathcal B$ are inserted. Equivalently it is an algebra object in the monoidal endomorphism category $\\mathcal B(A,A)$. This extends the ordinary-category definition in [[def-monad]]. It is **separable** if there is a $2$-morphism $\\sigma:T\\Rightarrow T\\circ T$ with\n$$\\mu\\cdot\\sigma=1_T,\\qquad \\sigma\\cdot\\mu=(\\mu\\ast1_T)\\cdot(1_T\\ast\\sigma)=(1_T\\ast\\mu)\\cdot(\\sigma\\ast1_T),$$\nagain with coherent associators. The last two equalities say that $\\sigma$ is a map of regular $T$-bimodules.\n\nAn **adjunction** $L\\dashv U$ in $\\mathcal B$, for $L:A\\to B$ and $U:B\\to A$, consists of $2$-morphisms $\\eta:1_A\\Rightarrow UL$ and $\\varepsilon:LU\\Rightarrow1_B$ with\n$$(\\varepsilon\\ast1_L)\\cdot(1_L\\ast\\eta)=1_L,\\qquad(1_U\\ast\\varepsilon)\\cdot(\\eta\\ast1_U)=1_U,$$\nwith associators and unitors inserted. These are the same triangle equations as in [[def-adjunction-by-unit-counit-and-triangle-identities]], now for $1$- and $2$-morphisms of $\\mathcal B$. Its induced monad is $(UL,\\eta,1_U\\ast\\varepsilon\\ast1_L)$.\n\nA **finite semisimple linear 2-category** is a weak linear 2-category in which:\n\n- every hom category is a finite semisimple $k$-linear category (it has finitely many simple isomorphism classes, finite-dimensional hom spaces, and every object is a finite direct sum of simples);\n- there is a zero object, and each pair $A,B$ has a biproduct $A\\oplus B$ with inclusions and projections $\\iota_A,\\iota_B,\\pi_A,\\pi_B$ satisfying $\\pi_i\\circ\\iota_j\\cong0$ for $i\\ne j$, $\\pi_i\\circ\\iota_i\\cong1$, and $\\iota_A\\circ\\pi_A\\oplus\\iota_B\\circ\\pi_B\\cong1_{A\\oplus B}$; finite biproducts are preserved by composition;\n- every 1-morphism has both a left and a right adjoint ([[def-adjunction-by-unit-counit-and-triangle-identities]]), with unit and counit 2-morphisms satisfying the two triangle identities;\n- every separable monad has a separable splitting: a separable monad $T:A\\to A$ is represented by an object $A_T$ and an adjunction $L\\dashv U$, where $L:A\\to A_T$ and $U:A_T\\to A$, in the sense of [[def-adjunction-by-unit-counit-and-triangle-identities]]. Its induced monad $UL$ is isomorphic to $T$ as a monad, and its counit $\\varepsilon:LU\\Rightarrow 1_{A_T}$ has a section $s:1_{A_T}\\Rightarrow LU$ as a 2-morphism in $\\mathcal B(A_T,A_T)$, with $\\varepsilon\\circ s=1_{1_{A_T}}$ (in a bimodule presentation this section is a bimodule map);\n- there are finitely many equivalence classes of simple objects, where an object $A$ is **simple** when its identity 1-morphism is simple in $\\mathcal B(A,A)$.\n\nThe splitting condition concerns monads in the 2-category; it is stronger than the existence of finite direct sums in each hom category. A choice of separable splitting is not part of the property.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": [
            {
              "title": "Douglas and Reutter, Fusion 2-Categories and a State-Sum Invariant for 4-Manifolds, §§1.4.1-1.4.3",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.11933"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "def-modc-relative-deligne-product",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Relative Deligne product in the braided module 2-category",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-semisimple-module-category-over-fusion-category",
            "def-braided-monoidal-category"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal C$ be a braided fusion category and let $\\mathcal M,\\mathcal N$ be finite semisimple right $\\mathcal C$-module categories. A **relative Deligne product** $\\mathcal M\\boxtimes_{\\mathcal C}\\mathcal N$ in $\\operatorname{Mod}(\\mathcal C)$ means a finite semisimple right $\\mathcal C$-module category $\\mathcal P$ equipped with an exact bilinear functor $b:\\mathcal M\\times\\mathcal N\\to\\mathcal P$ that is balanced over the middle $\\mathcal C$-action and compatible with the outer right $\\mathcal C$-action, and is universal among such functors: for every finite semisimple right $\\mathcal C$-module category $\\mathcal D$, composition with $b$ gives an equivalence between right $\\mathcal C$-module functors $\\mathcal P\\to\\mathcal D$ and exact bilinear middle-balanced right $\\mathcal C$-module functors $\\mathcal M\\times\\mathcal N\\to\\mathcal D$.\n\nFor the balancing convention, a right module category $\\mathcal N$ is regarded as a left module category by $X\\triangleright n:=n\\odot X$. Its left module associator $(X\\otimes Y)\\triangleright n\\to X\\triangleright(Y\\triangleright n)$ is the composite of $n\\odot c_{Y,X}^{-1}$ with the inverse right-module associator. “Balanced” means that $b(m\\odot X,n)\\cong b(m,X\\triangleright n)$ naturally in all variables, compatibly with the unit and associativity constraints. This definition fixes the universal property and the braiding convention; it does not assert that a product exists.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": [
