        },
        {
          "id": "def-opposite-algebra-in-braided-fusion-category",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Opposite algebra induced by a braiding",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-braided-monoidal-category",
            "def-separable-algebra-and-bimodule-in-fusion-category"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $(\\mathcal C,\\otimes,\\mathbf1,c)$ be a braided monoidal category and let $(A,m,\\eta)$ be an algebra object ([[def-separable-algebra-and-bimodule-in-fusion-category]]). Define the monoidal opposite $\\mathcal C^{\\mathrm{mp}}$ to have the same objects and morphisms, tensor product $X\\otimes_{\\mathrm{mp}}Y:=Y\\otimes X$, associator $\\alpha^{\\mathrm{mp}}_{X,Y,Z}:=\\alpha^{-1}_{Z,Y,X}$, and unitors $\\lambda^{\\mathrm{mp}}_X:=\\rho_X$, $\\rho^{\\mathrm{mp}}_X:=\\lambda_X$. The braiding defines a strong monoidal equivalence $J:\\mathcal C^{\\mathrm{mp}}\\to\\mathcal C$ that is the identity on the underlying category and has tensorator $c_{Y,X}^{-1}:X\\otimes Y\\to Y\\otimes X$.\n\nRegard $A$ as an algebra in $\\mathcal C^{\\mathrm{mp}}$ with the same unit and multiplication $m:A\\otimes_{\\mathrm{mp}}A=A\\otimes A\\to A$. Its transport along $J$ is the **opposite algebra** $A^{\\mathrm{op}}$ in $\\mathcal C$; its multiplication is\n$$m_{A^{\\mathrm{op}}}=m\\circ c_{A,A}^{-1}:A\\otimes A\\to A,$$\nand its unit is $\\eta$. This convention fixes the braiding direction. The hexagon identities are the tensorator coherence of $J$, and its unit coherence follows from the braiding unit equations.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
